#show: setup.with(title: "Differential - M1 Problem statement", short: "M1 · Problem statement")

#title-block(
  title: [Differential: simulation-grounded AI diagnosis for analog audio electronics],
  subtitle: "Milestone 1 · Problem statement",
  meta: ("PRV 201 Frontiers of Technology (AI)", "Track A: Build", "Stevens Institute of Technology", "September 2026"),
)
*Executive summary.* When a tube preamplifier, studio console or vintage hi-fi unit fails, a technician must find which of its dozens to hundreds of parts failed; specialist shops we sampled charge \$125 to \$150 for that evaluation \[1, 2, 3\]. Differential assists the search. For a circuit it has a model of (@fig:concept), it simulates how every cataloged part failure changes the readings, keeps a probability for every suspect and recommends the measurement expected to narrow them most per unit of effort; a language model reads the complaint and explains but never supplies a reading, and safety rules run in code. Success, fixed before any test unit is run, means naming the failed part first on at least 90% of 1,000 simulated units and on at least 49 of 60 faults inserted into real circuit boards, beating the best scripted procedure and a chat model, using at most 0.35 times an expert's probing effort, stating honest probabilities, and sending at most 11% of units outside its fault list to a healthy part. All evidence so far is simulated; the boards and a technician study will test transfer and real benefit. Scope: single static faults in preamplifier and line-level circuits.

#figure(
  image("/results/figures/concept.svg", width: 83%),
  caption: [Differential in one picture. Offline, each circuit and its fault catalog are simulated with SPICE (circuit-simulation software) under part tolerances and learned as fault signatures. Online, a Bayesian loop picks each measurement; the language model (LLM) reads the complaint, proposes meter-photo readings for confirmation and explains. Safety rules and the check that every number traces to a measurement run in code.],
) <fig:concept>

#part(1, "Problem, stakeholders and industry context", "MEMBER_1")

*The problem.* Studio consoles, tube preamplifiers and vintage hi-fi are repaired long after their makers stop supporting them, and finding the failed part is usually the slow step: for heavy equipment, a trade article puts diagnosing an electrical fault at four hours or more against a ten-minute repair \[4\]; for audio, our interviews will measure it. Three specialist shops we sampled charge \$125 to \$150 for a bench evaluation, one covering the first hour of repair and one credited against it \[1, 2, 3\]; another bills a one-hour minimum even when no fault is found \[5\]. These are examples, not a survey.

*A small, specialized workforce.* The closest U.S. occupation, audiovisual equipment installers and repairers (24,720 people in May 2023 \[6\]), is projected to grow faster than average \[7\] and is not old (median age 38.4 in 2017 \[8\]); the scarcity is narrower, as a 2026 trade report describes informally trained vintage-audio specialists, many near retirement, and repair queues of months \[9\]. An AI assistant raised support agents' productivity 14% on average and 34% for novices \[10\]; if that holds at the bench, newer technicians gain most, so the tool must support their judgment, not replace it.

*Where it pays.* A new circuit needs its schematic transcribed (we will time one) and, for our 59-part channel strip, about 75 minutes of computer time; that is repaid fastest where one model serves many units, such as a console of identical channel strips or a popular receiver.

*Where current AI stands.* Maintenance AI retrieves documented knowledge: service histories (PTC ServiceMax AI \[11\]), manuals (Microsoft's field-service Copilot \[12\]), predictive-maintenance guidance (Siemens \[13\]), agentic workflows (IBM Maximo 9.2, June 2026 \[14\]) and repair guides (iFixit's FixBot \[15\]). None simulates the circuit on the bench. The best chat model scores 95.50% on the September 2026 Razavi-Bench of textbook analog questions, graded by another language model \[16\], but at the bench parts sit anywhere within tolerance, faults look alike, measurements cost effort and some points carry lethal voltage. A research system raised its base model from 79.52% to 97.59% on college circuit problems with a diagram detector and a simulation loop that re-checks answers \[17\]; Differential builds on such a check.

*Rules that apply.* The EU AI Act would not class a bench aid as high-risk: no Annex I law requires it to pass third-party assessment, and no Annex III area applies unless shops rated technicians or trainees with it \[18\]. Users must be told they are dealing with an AI from 2 August 2026 \[18, 19\]; the 2026 amendments moved the Annex III rules to 2 December 2027 \[20\] and keep a duty to support staff's AI literacy \[21\], which our training material serves.

