#decision("The primary user is a qualified bench technician")[
*Options:* a consumer self-repair assistant or a professional bench tool. *Choice:* professional, because diagnosis often means testing a powered, open chassis. OSHA's rules, which protect employees, require live parts to be de-energized unless testing needs power (parts under 50 V to ground excepted, if that adds no burn or arc-blast exposure) and let only qualified persons, trained in the equipment and its hazards, work on energized parts \[22, 23\] or test circuits \[24\]; 5 of the channel strip's 23 test points can exceed 50 V (@tab:library). *Consequence:* licensed to repair businesses for qualified technicians; trainees work supervised; mains-side work is refused. Hobbyists keep forums and chat models, which have no safety rules.
]

#part(2, "How diagnosis is done today, and the data landscape", "MEMBER_2")

*Today's practice.* The usual sequence, which our interviews will check: confirm the complaint; look for burnt or bulging parts; check the supplies; follow a test tone stage by stage, often halving the search ("half-split tracing"); compare voltages with the manual's chart or the healthy channel; test tubes and electrolytics with a tube tester or ESR meter; check service bulletins; then substitute or unsolder a suspect and confirm the fault clears. The hard judgment is which reading is really "off", since parts sit anywhere within tolerance and old ones drift beyond it: drying electrolytics lose capacitance and gain series resistance \[25\]. Replacing every electrolytic ("recapping") never finds the failed part.

*The information is uneven.* Service manuals with schematics and voltage charts exist for many classic models, but units are often modified or undocumented. U.S. state repair laws cover only products first sold after 2015 or 2021, several only digital electronics \[26, 27, 28, 29, 30, 31\], and the EU's obligation to repair covers only the product groups in its Annex II, such as washing machines and displays \[32, 33\]: vintage analog gear falls outside all of them. Differential needs a circuit model per product, built from the schematic, and trusts its hazard map only once the model's healthy readings match the manual's chart. Modified units are what "no single fault fits" is for; units without a schematic are out of scope.

*Labeled fault data is scarce, so we simulate it.* Confirming a failed part takes unsoldering and is rarely recorded; shop logs are private and, after recapping, unlabeled; manuals give only healthy values. We know of no public dataset of bench readings with confirmed failed parts; published methods use benchmark filter circuits \[34\] and simulated data \[35\]. The analog-test literature built simulated fault dictionaries \[36\], showed that some faults cannot be told apart at the available test points, forming ambiguity groups \[37\], and applied machine learning \[34, 38\] to drift faults that tolerances hide \[35\]; we follow that path (@tab:library). A simulated unit is one draw of every part within its tolerance, the mains within ±5%, and one cataloged fault (an open, a short, a drift, or a part-specific mode such as a leaky transistor or a worn tube), and every reading carries a real instrument's error: a 10 MΩ meter loads each DC node \[39\] and reads within ±(0.5% + 2 counts) \[40\], an oscilloscope within ±3% \[41\]. The library gave 253,000 units, 0 failing to converge. Hand calculations match every simulated bias point and gain within 0.5-5% or 0.1 dB (22 of 22 checks). Fault behavior has not been checked fault by fault (one close look exposed a simulator artifact, since fixed), so real boards are the systematic check. A complaint alone is weak evidence: on the channel strip a hum complaint fits 25 faults, low output 21, and 119 faults usually cause none.

