*How it will be measured.* The test set holds 1,000 single-fault units (500 on the channel strip, 100 per block), seeds disjoint from training. Each case draws a fault uniformly from the catalog and keeps the unit only if it shows a symptom, else draws another fault, so faults count by how often they cause a symptom, not how often they fail; old electrolytics and tubes fail far more often than film resistors, so T14 also reweights to a capacitor-heavy mix. Complaints come from a phrase bank the reader was not developed on (its overall score was seen once, before the rules last changed; T18 uses a sealed second bank), in three voices, never name a part, and drop each symptom with probability 20% and add each other symptom with 2%. Stress sets: 500 units outside the catalog (70% double faults, 30% a wrong-value part or a solder bridge); 300 aged units (electrolytics up to 30% low with up to 3× their series resistance, resistors up to 10% high, tubes up to 30% weaker); tolerances 1.5, 2 and 3 times wider. The scripts, both engines and the full system (@tab:methods) diagnose every unit; the scripts share the engine's inference and stopping rule and unsolder their leading suspect at 50% belief, so each margin isolates how measurements are chosen. A language model works alone, and with our simulator as a tool (no bar), on a stratified 300-unit subset with the same information, three sessions per unit.

*Hardware and people.* For T7 a teammate who does not record inserts 60 faults, in a sealed random order, into three 24 V driver boards (one of used parts); each test point is read once per fault and every method replayed on the recordings. Study S1 (pre-registered, no bar) will time at least six technicians with and without the tool, after ethics review.

*Pilots set the bars; no test unit has been run.* Bars come from a pilot on units simulated for it alone (200 channel strip, 60 per block, 250 out-of-model, 150 aged), after two earlier pilots exposed problems since fixed. Weighted like the test set (channel strip one half, each block one tenth), the full system named the right group first on 92.1% of pilot units, naming a group on 93.2% and right on 98.8% of those. The engine alone, ignoring the complaint, was right on 93.8%: the noisy complaints saved 4.8% of the effort but cost 1.8 points of accuracy, so the complaint reader has yet to prove itself (T11, T18). On the channel strip the engine alone was right on 94.0% at a mean effort of 6.7, half-split tracing on 83.5% at 22.9, the chart on 77.0% at 24.1 and random probing on 80.5% at 23.0. Each bar is the pilot estimate moved 1.5 standard errors against the system and rounded; because the confidence bound must clear it, a primary can be missed even if the system matches the pilot, and a miss will be reported as one.

*Scope.* In: single static faults (opens, shorts, drift, wear) and the hum and distortion they cause, in supplies, preamplifier and line-level stages. Out: power amplifiers (another catalog), intermittent and mechanical faults (static simulation cannot produce them), double faults (flagged only), mains-side work, units without a schematic, unattended probing.

#decision("Score by ambiguity group and weighted effort")[
*Options:* per-part accuracy and a count of measurements, or group accuracy and weighted effort. *Choice:* the latter: per-part scoring rewards lucky guesses between faults with identical readings, and unsoldering a part is not like touching a probe. *Consequence:* group sizes are reported (@tab:library) and effort targets use weighted units.
]

#part(4, "Preliminary AI approach, risks and ethics", "MEMBER_4")

*Why a hybrid.* Language models guess when uncertain, partly because training and evaluation reward guessing \[42\]; the 97.59% system in Part 1 — Problem, stakeholders and industry context needed a simulation check. Simulation supplies the physics; Bayesian inference keeps a probability for every suspect, "no single fault fits" included, updated after each reading through likelihoods learned from the simulated units: one to three Gaussians per fault over the circuit's readings (51 on the channel strip, 23 of them DC voltages), with boosted trees \[43\] as a comparison engine (weighted pilot top-1: 93.8% and 92.8%). The next measurement maximizes expected information gain per unit of effort, as in sequential fault diagnosis \[44\]; a diagnosis stops at 90% belief in one group, at the budget, or when nothing informative remains. The language model reads complaints (offline, a rule-based reader does), proposes meter-photo readings for confirmation and explains each step; every number it writes must trace to a reading (T17). It stays only if T5, T18 and T19 show it helps.

